MilkDropper for Debian/Ubuntu
=============================

Desktop (wallpaper) mode requires an OpenGL scene graph
-------------------------------------------------------

The wallpaper renderer is a QQuickFramebufferObject, which only works when
Plasma's Qt Quick scene graph runs on the OpenGL RHI backend. If Plasma is
running on Vulkan (the default on some drivers, and whatever is recorded in
kdeglobals), the wallpaper renders nothing at all — silently.

Check the current setting:

    kreadconfig6 --file kdeglobals --group QtQuickRendererSettings \
        --key SceneGraphBackend

Switch it to OpenGL and restart the shell:

    kwriteconfig6 --file kdeglobals --group QtQuickRendererSettings \
        --key SceneGraphBackend opengl
    systemctl --user restart plasma-plasmashell.service

This is a session-wide setting: every KDE Qt Quick application switches
backend with it, not just the wallpaper. Note that the QSG_RHI_BACKEND
environment variable does NOT work here — KDE's platform theme calls
QQuickWindow::setGraphicsApi(), which takes precedence over it.

Standard mode (a projectMSDL window) is unaffected and works on any backend.

Why a private libprojectM
-------------------------

Qt Quick hands out OpenGL ES contexts on Wayland, and a desktop-GL build of
libprojectM refuses to initialise on those. MilkDropper therefore links a build
made with ENABLE_GLES, installed under /usr/lib/milkdropper so it cannot shadow
a system libprojectM carrying the same SONAME. The plugin locates it by RPATH.

Presets and projectMSDL
-----------------------

Neither is shipped here. MilkDropper discovers them at runtime, in order:

  1. $MILKDROPPER_PRESET_PATH / $MILKDROPPER_TEXTURE_PATH / $MILKDROPPER_PROJECTMSDL
  2. ~/.config/milkdropper/milkdropper.conf
  3. well-known locations, including the Steam build of projectM

To pin them explicitly, create ~/.config/milkdropper/milkdropper.conf:

    [Paths]
    Presets=/path/to/presets
    Textures=/path/to/textures
    ProjectMSDL=/path/to/projectMSDL

Run `python3 /usr/share/milkdropper/milkdropper_paths.py` to see what is found.
